---
import BenchPage from '../../layouts/BenchPage.astro';
import FlavorBloom from '../../components/coffee/FlavorBloom.svelte';
import originHubs from '../../data/origin-hubs.json';
import { FAMILIES, OFF_GROUPS, ALL_NOTES, inSample } from '../../lib/coffee/flavors.ts';
import { ORIGINS } from '../../lib/coffee/origins.ts';
import { EXPLORE_SOURCES } from '../../lib/coffee/explore-sources.ts';

const title = 'Coffee Flavor Bloom: Tasting Notes, Explained';
const description = 'Pick a tasting note like blueberry, jasmine or caramel to learn what it tastes like, how to train your nose on it, and which origins and processes roasters use the word for.';
// Roasters' lot descriptions (winter 2026 catalogs): only the fields the bloom counts.
// Lots with a description, single origins only (inSample).
const lots = originHubs.flatMap((h) => h.beans.filter((b) => inSample(b.name, b.flavor_notes)).map((b) => ({ o: h.origin_slug.replace(/_/g, '-'), p: b.processing, r: b.roast_level, notes: b.flavor_notes })));
const origins = ORIGINS.map((o) => ({ id: o.id, name: o.name, page: o.page }));
const cited = ['sca-wcr-wheel', 'wcr-lexicon', 'sca-cva', 'easto', 'synthesis', 'roaster-lots-2026'].map((id) => EXPLORE_SOURCES[id]);
const noteCount = ALL_NOTES.length;
const offCount = OFF_GROUPS.reduce((t, g) => t + g.notes.length, 0);
---

<BenchPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  kicker="Explore · Flavor Bloom"
  lede="Every note on a bag is a word you can learn to taste. Pick one to find out how."
  back={{ href: '/explore/', label: 'The gallery' }}
  gallery
  schema={[{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Flavor Bloom',
    url: 'https://jayarr.coffee/explore/flavors/',
    description,
    applicationCategory: 'EducationalApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'JayArr Coffee', url: 'https://jayarr.coffee' },
  }]}
>
  <Fragment slot="title">Flavor Bloom: <em>learn to taste a note</em></Fragment>
  <FlavorBloom client:load lots={lots} origins={origins} />
  <noscript><p class="bp-noscript">The interactive bloom needs JavaScript. Every family and note is listed below.</p></noscript>

  <Fragment slot="body">
    <h2>How the bloom works</h2>
    <p>The eight petals are the flavor families of the SCA and World Coffee Research Coffee Taster's Flavor Wheel. Open one and its groups fan out; pick a note and you get three different kinds of information, kept apart on purpose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Roasters say:</strong> how often roasters use the word, counted from {lots.length} lot descriptions in 38 specialty roasters' catalogs, recorded January–March 2026. It shows where a word tends to turn up, by process, roast and origin. It isn't a measurement, and it can't promise what a particular bag will taste like.</li>
      <li><strong>Tends to:</strong> general tendencies, such as lighter roasts keeping more fruit and acidity, with their sources.</li>
      <li><strong>You noticed:</strong> your own tasting card. Add up to three notes you can taste, type in what the bag says, and see where you agree. The card stays on your device.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to taste for a note</h2>
    <p>Most of what we call flavor is smell reaching the nose from the back of the mouth. Smell the reference first (a crushed blueberry, a strip of lemon zest, a square of dark chocolate), then sip the coffee, swallow and breathe out through your nose. Look for the same smell, not the same taste: coffee that "tastes of blueberry" doesn't taste like a blueberry.</p>
    <p>Acids are the exception. Citric and malic acid are tasted on the tongue, so those exercises use a pinch of citric acid in water or a tart apple.</p>
    <p>Taste the same cup again as it cools. The SCA's cupping protocol has tasters return to each cup several times from about 70 °C down, because what you notice changes.</p>

    <h2>Off notes</h2>
    <p>The leaves on the stem hold the wheel's "other" notes: papery, musty, phenolic and rubbery tastes, and the raw-potato defect found in some East African lots. Faint, a few (earthy, woody) can be part of a coffee's character; strong or persistent, they usually point to a problem on the farm, in processing, in storage or with age. Roasting doesn't fix a defect, though a darker roast can hide some of it.</p>

    <h2>Every note on the bloom</h2>
    <p class="bp-note">{noteCount} notes: {noteCount - offCount} in eight families and {offCount} off notes. Notes marked * aren't printed on the wheel in this form: they're words roasters use often, or the wheel's notes combined, placed in the nearest group. Each note counts only its own words, listed on its card.</p>
    {FAMILIES.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3>{f.label}</h3>
        <ul>{f.groups.map((g) => <li><strong>{g.label}:</strong> {g.notes.map((n) => n.label + (n.wheel ? '' : '*')).join(', ')}</li>)}</ul>
      </>
    ))}
    <h3>Off notes</h3>
    <ul>{OFF_GROUPS.map((g) => <li><strong>{g.label}:</strong> {g.notes.map((n) => n.label + (n.wheel ? '' : '*')).join(', ')}</li>)}</ul>

    <h2>Keep going</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/explore/origins/">Origins atlas</a>: where the coffees roasters describe this way grow, and when they're harvested.</li>
      <li><a href="/explore/methods/">Brewing Constellations</a>: why a paper filter shows delicate notes and a press adds body.</li>
      <li><a href="/tools/fix-my-coffee/">Fix My Coffee</a>: when the cup tastes sour, bitter or flat instead.</li>
      <li><a href="/blog/coffee-scoring-system-explained/">How coffee is scored</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="bp-sources">
      {cited.map((s) => <li>{s.author}, <cite>{s.title}</cite>{s.year ? ` (${s.year})` : ''}{s.where ? `: ${s.where}` : ''}.</li>)}
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</BenchPage>
